# Line endings, pinned rather than left to each machine's `core.autocrlf`.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS. Without it, git converts line endings on checkout using a per-machine
# setting, so the same file can be LF in one clone and CRLF in another. The damage is not
# corruption - it is that a diff then shows every line of a file as changed when one line was
# edited. On a repo where the next step is "review the code and demonstrate you understand
# it", a diff that says 400 lines changed when 3 did is actively harmful: it hides the real
# edit inside noise, and it makes a squash before release unreadable.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` means: git decides what is text, stores text as LF in history, and
# checks it out as LF everywhere. One representation, no per-machine drift.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows batch files are the exception and it is not cosmetic. cmd.exe can mis-parse a .cmd
# with LF endings - a label or a multi-line block silently does the wrong thing - so these
# must be CRLF on disk no matter what the rest of the tree does. All three of DRIP's
# double-clickable entry points are .cmd, and they are what the content owners actually use.
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Binaries: never convert, never diff as text. A 3 MB bundle rendered as a text diff is a
# hung terminal, and any "line ending fix" applied to one would corrupt it.
# `*.bundle` is gitignored as well - this is belt and braces, and it also covers a bundle
# that someone force-adds to a branch for a one-off comparison.
*.bundle  binary
*.resS    binary
*.png     binary
*.jpg     binary
*.dll     binary
*.pdb     binary
*.xlsx    binary

# Content and tooling are text and want normal diffs, stated explicitly so nothing here
# depends on git's heuristics guessing right about a .jsonc or a .json5 it has not seen.
*.jsonc   text
*.json    text
*.json5   text
*.py      text
*.cs      text
*.csproj  text
*.md      text
*.tsv     text
